      "AI plus discipline can change that.",
      "A simple practice: 1. Every closed-won and closed-lost deal above a certain size gets: - A short structured note: - Why we think we won/lost - Key factors (price, product fit, timing, relationship, competition)",
      "- A call transcript summary (if relevant) 1. AI helps by: - Drafting the first version based on emails, calls,",
      "CRM notes - Suggesting common patterns or tags 1. Periodically (monthly or quarterly), AI analyses: - Which factors show up most often in wins vs",
      "losses - Which segments respond best to which offers - Which competitors beat you where 1. Sales leadership: - Reviews these patterns - Updates messaging, strategy, playbooks - Feeds back into lead scoring and targeting You stop telling yourself stories and start seeing reality.",
      "What AI Cannot Do in Sales (And You Shouldn't Ask It To)",
      "AI is powerful, but there are clear boundaries you should respect.",
      "AI cannot:",
      "- Build trust and long-term relationships with key accounts",
      "- Take responsibility when things go wrong - Truly understand the politics and subtext in",
      "complex deals - Replace the judgment of a seasoned salesperson",
      "in high-stakes negotiations",
      "If you try to turn your salesforce into prompt operators and AI parrots, you will:",
      "- Damage your brand - Lose subtle opportunities - Demotivate your best people Use AI to: - Remove rubbish work - Bring insight and structure - Standardise what can be standardised Then invest your human effort in: - Relationship - Strategy - Complex problem-solving - Creative deal-making",
      "Common Failure Modes in AI-for-Sales (So You Don't Fall for Them)",
      "1. Tool sprawl - Every rep uses different AI tools: one for email,",
      "one for notes, one for LinkedIn messages. - Nothing is integrated with the CRM. 1. Result: - Data goes missing - Management has no visibility - Reps waste time context switching 1. Counter: - Choose a small, curated stack. - Integrate with CRM.",
      "- Sunset tools that don't talk to your core systems. 1. Garbage in, AI on top - CRM data is partial and wrong. - Reps skip fields or game them. - Then you build models on this. 1. Result: - Wrong scores, bad recommendations - Reps stop trusting the system 1. Counter: - Clean core data first. - Enforce a minimal set of required fields. - Use AI to help reps fill data (e.g., auto-log calls",
      "and emails), not to replace discipline. 1. Over-automation of customer",
      "communication - AI sends too many robotic emails. - Customers sense the lack of authenticity. - Response rates drop. 1. Counter: - Use AI for first drafts and routine touches. - Keep human-crafted communication for high-",
      "value interactions. - Monitor complaints and unsubscribes closely. 1. Ignoring rep experience - Leadership buys tools. - Reps feel monitored and constrained, not",
      "empowered. - Adoption is low, gaming is high. 1. Counter:",
      "- Involve reps in design and selection. - Show them how it makes their life easier. - Give them some control and input on how agents",
      "behave.",
      "What a Sane AI-Enhanced Sales Machine Looks Like",
      "Let's put it together.",
      "In a reasonably mature setup:",
      "- Memory - Every call, email and meeting is captured in a",
      "usable way - Key insights and decisions per account are",
      "summarised - Win/loss reasons are consistently recorded - Reasoning - Lead and account scores help prioritise effort - Forecasts start from model baselines, then",
      "incorporate human judgment - Patterns in wins/losses and segment behaviour",
      "are regularly analysed - Action - Workflows trigger follow-ups, reminders, and",
      "status updates - Agents handle routine messages in lower-value",
      "segments - Reps focus their time on high-value",
      "conversations and complex deals - Feedback",
      "- Experiments in messaging, cadence, targeting are tracked",
      "- Lessons update playbooks, templates and agent behaviours",
      "- The sales machine gets a little sharper each quarter",
