      "The only unacceptable outcome is \"we're not sure, so let's vaguely keep it alive forever\".",
      "Step 13: Codify the Playbook, Not the Tool",
      "No matter which way you decide, you now document how you did it, so you can repeat it.",
      "One short internal doc:",
      "\"How we design, build and govern AI use-cases in this company (v1.0)\"",
      "Sections:",
      "- Choosing a value stream - Selecting a use-case - Defining metrics - Mapping Memory/Reasoning/Action/Feedback - Building pilots - Governance and risk categories - Red-button and incident handling - Review cadence This doesn't have to be polished. It has to be true. That's your operating manual for the next round.",
      "The Leadership Habits You Need During These 90 Days",
      "Tools and teams won't save you if you show up the same way you always have. A few behaviours to intentionally practice:",
      "1. Ask better questions in every meeting - \"What learning are we getting from this?\" - \"What data would actually change our mind",
      "here?\" - \"Where can we automate the stupid parts of this",
      "process?\" - \"What decision are we taking, and who owns it?\" 1. Kill two things for every one you start - Old reports no one reads - Vanity dashboards - Zombie projects",
      "1. Free up attention and capacity. AI doesn't run on hope. It runs on time, focus, and painful trade-offs.",
      "2. Refuse theatre - No pilots just for press releases - No vendor showcases without a real business",
      "problem - No \"AI task force\" that never touches a live",
      "process 1. If something doesn't connect to a real value",
      "stream, treat it as entertainment, not transformation. 2. Narrate the change relentlessly - To your team: - \"Here's why we're doing this.\" - \"Here's what will change for you.\" - \"Here's what won't.\" 1. People can handle truth. They revolt against spin and surprises. 2. Protect a small circle of reality-tellers - Your skunkworks spine - A couple of frontline sceptics - One or two brutally honest managers 1. Listen when they say, \"This is not working,\" or \"This is gold, we're under-investing.\"",
      "Common Ways a 90-Day Push Goes Off the Rails",
      "You can dodge a lot of pain by recognising these early.",
      "1. Scope creep",
      "- You start with one use-case. - Three weeks in, you're trying to fix the entire",
      "CRM and rewrite the data warehouse. - Result: nothing ships. 1. Antidote: - Write the scope down. - Protect it. - Start a \"next round\" list for good ideas that don't",
      "fit. 1. Tool obsession - Meetings become vendor comparison sessions. - Architecture diagrams multiply. - Actual working prototypes lag. 1. Antidote: - Prototype with what you have. - Swap tools later if needed. Impact first,",
      "aesthetics later. 1. Leadership absenteeism - You sponsor the initiative formally, then vanish. - Decisions get stuck. - Politics fills the gap. 1. Antidote: - Put recurring 30-minute check-ins in your own",
      "calendar. - Show up. Ask hard questions. Clear roadblocks. 1. Governance as performance, not practice - You create a steering group and a register. - Nobody uses them. - AI systems proliferate anyway.",
      "1. Antidote: - Refuse to allow new significant AI use-cases",
      "without being on the register. - Keep the governance spine light, but real. 1. Fear-based freeze - First minor incident happens. - Everyone panics. - Informal ban on \"doing anything risky\" appears. 1. Antidote: - Treat incidents as training data, not as proof",
      "that AI is evil. - Pause, fix, continue. Just as you would for any",
      "other important system.",
      "Flight Exercise: Your 90-Day AI",
      "Leadership Charter (One Page to Rule the Chaos)",
      "Before you close this book, write a one-page charter you could email to your leadership team tomorrow morning. No slides. No slogans. Just a blunt statement of intent. Four sections, short and sharp:",
      "1. Our Focus for the Next 90 Days",
      "\"For the next 90 days, our AI focus will be: - Value stream: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - First use-case: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - Target metrics: - _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ from _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ to _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ from _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ to _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _.\"",
      "2. The Team",
      "\"The core team for this 90-day push is: - Value stream owner: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - Data/AI lead: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - Tech/ops lead: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - Frontline representative: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ - Governance lead: _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _.\"",
      "\"And I ( ________ ) will personally review progress every ________.\"",
      "3. How We'll Work",
      "\"We will: - Prototype in 30-40 days, not 6 months. - Pilot in a limited scope, with logs and guardrails. - Measure against the target metrics, honestly. - Decide by Day 90 whether to scale, adjust, or kill.\" \"We will not: - Run pilots that never touch real work. - Launch systems without clear owners and red-buttons. - Pretend vanity metrics are success.\"",
      "4. What This Is Really About",
      "\"This is not a side project. This is the first step in rewiring how our company thinks, decides, and works. If we get this right, we will: - Serve customers faster and better. - Free our people from pointless work. - Make better bets with less drama. - Stay dangerous in a world where everyone has access to the same tools - but not the same discipline. Ninety days from now, we will either have done that, or we will know exactly why we haven't. I am not interested in the third option: 'nothing really changed.'\"",
      "Sign it.",
      "Then act like you meant it.",
      "Closing",
      "You don't need to become an AI researcher. You don't need to understand every model architecture. You don't need a 200-page \"transformation roadmap\".",
